<mujoco model="graspable_carton">
  <!-- A small upright carton, sized to be picked off a TABLE by a NARROW parallel gripper.

       The sibling of `graspable_box`, and it exists because that one cannot serve this case. Two
       independent constraints have to be met at once and a single asset cannot be scaled to meet
       both:

         * ACROSS THE JAWS the object must fit the gripper's aperture. `graspable_box` is 60 mm,
           sized from the Unitree Dex1's measured 5.9..94.9 mm range. A PAL PRO gripper's pad inner
           faces open to a measured 59.7 mm, so a 60 mm object has ~0 mm of clearance: it cannot be
           brought down around it at all.
         * IN HEIGHT the object must be tall enough that the gripper's own body clears the tabletop
           while the finger PADS are alongside it. `spawn_model`'s `scale` is uniform, so shrinking
           `graspable_box` enough to leave real clearance also shrinks its HEIGHT -- at scale 0.5 it is
           25 mm tall, and then the gripper base bottoms out on the table with only ~9 mm of pad/object
           overlap. Measured on the TIAGo Pro: pads at z 0.733..0.771 with the base resting at 0.716
           on a 0.717 m table.

       Hence a separate prop whose proportions are chosen rather than scaled: 24 mm across the jaws
       and 90 mm tall, so the pads engage its middle with ~50 mm of clearance under the gripper. 40 mm
       deep so it stands rather than topples, and light enough not to disturb a mobile base's balance.

       A top-down grasp has to bring OPEN pads down either side of the object, so the usable placement
       tolerance is (aperture - width)/2. At 24 mm in a 59.7 mm gripper that is ~18 mm per side, which a
       drive-align-then-IK pipeline can hold; the same pipeline delivers ~10 mm of lateral error, so
       the margin is real but not generous. If you scale this prop up, check that budget first.

       Measure the aperture over the FINGERTIP pads only, and restricted to the depth the object
       occupies. Taking min/max over the whole finger assembly gives 10.9 mm, because the four-bar
       linkage bars converge near the palm -- nowhere the object ever reaches. That number is what made
       an earlier version of this comment claim a 41 mm gripper.

       Contact parameters are inherited verbatim from `graspable_box`, for the reasons its own
       comment gives: a box primitive rather than a mesh (a convex hull's edge normals are what make
       a pinched object squirt out of the pads); condim="4" for torsional friction, without which a
       held object rotates out of the grasp about the grasp axis under its own weight; sliding
       friction 1.2 for cardboard-on-rubber; and softened solimp/solref so the pads settle into a
       few tenths of a millimetre instead of chattering at a 2 ms timestep.

       Spawn it with `spawn_model: {model: graspable_carton, free: true, publish_tf: dynamic}` --
       `free` adds the <freejoint/>, so this file declares none and the same prop can also be placed
       as static scenery. See the tiago_pick example (robovast-examples) for a worked pick. -->
  <default>
    <geom condim="4" friction="1.2 0.005 0.0001" solimp="0.9 0.95 0.001" solref="0.005 1"/>
  </default>

  <asset>
    <material name="graspable_carton" rgba="0.72 0.55 0.36 1"/>
  </asset>

  <worldbody>
    <!-- 0.04 x 0.024 x 0.09 m, 0.2 kg. GRASP DIMENSION IS Y (24 mm). Mass sits on the geom so
         `spawn_model`'s `mass:` override has a single value to rescale. -->
    <body name="graspable_carton">
      <geom name="graspable_carton" type="box" size="0.02 0.012 0.045" mass="0.2"
            material="graspable_carton"/>
    </body>
  </worldbody>
</mujoco>
